WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.157 --> 00:00:02.390
- Melbourne! We're actually
leaving Melbourne now.

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:06.067
- We are on our way to the
wine regions of Victoria.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:07.273
- Victoria!

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:08.462
Goodbye to the city.

00:00:08.462 --> 00:00:10.165
(kissing sound)

00:00:10.165 --> 00:00:11.774
- [Steven] Check it out.
- [Andrew] World War I.

00:00:11.774 --> 00:00:14.444
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:17.598
- We are now in part two
of a three part series,

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:19.187
we're the Worth It Hungry Boys.

00:00:19.187 --> 00:00:20.349
- Well today we're thirsty boys.

00:00:20.349 --> 00:00:21.591
- Taking on Oz.

00:00:21.591 --> 00:00:23.730
- Today on Worth It
Wine, Australia edition,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.506
we're going to be trying
three bottles of wine,

00:00:25.506 --> 00:00:27.297
at three drastically
different price points,

00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:29.849
to find out which wine is
the most worthy at its price.

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:31.421
- So I did some more
independent research--

00:00:31.421 --> 00:00:32.254
- Uh huh

00:00:32.254 --> 00:00:33.550
- About Australian drinking culture.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.013
I learned from our driver, Sam,

00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:37.523
an alternative to saying "cheers."

00:00:37.523 --> 00:00:39.658
This one is a little
bit more of a thinker.

00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:40.491
Up yours.

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:43.188
- That's not a thinker it's uh--

00:00:43.188 --> 00:00:44.189
- It's a stinker.

00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:45.947
(laughing)

00:00:45.947 --> 00:00:48.696
(lively guitar music)

00:00:48.696 --> 00:00:50.543
- My name is Alister Purbrick and I'm

00:00:50.543 --> 00:00:51.973
a fourth generation wine maker.

00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:54.005
We're standing in the Tahbilk Winery,

00:00:54.005 --> 00:00:56.273
which began its operations back in 1860.

00:00:56.273 --> 00:00:58.303
Today we're gonna be
talking about Marsanne,

00:00:58.303 --> 00:01:00.189
and in particular, the 2016 vintage.

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:01.606
We have 100 acres, which is

00:01:01.606 --> 00:01:03.200
the largest single holding in the world.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.664
We make our Marsanne differently to most

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:07.614
in that it is done with steel, fermented.

00:01:07.614 --> 00:01:10.229
The critical factor is
about excluding oxygen

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:11.977
over every step of the process.

00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:13.498
That's the only way that you'll be able

00:01:13.498 --> 00:01:16.428
to showcase the diversity of
flavors that the variety has.

00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:18.611
The wine enters the
bottle and the gas cover,

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.754
and then it just runs around
and gets labeled into boxes,

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:22.222
stacked away, ready for sale.

00:01:22.222 --> 00:01:24.922
- In the case over there we
saw a spectrum of Marsanne's

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:26.918
that are getting darker
as the bottle is older.

00:01:26.918 --> 00:01:28.740
Can you tell us what is
happening with those?

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.126
- [Alister] That's just the
normal oxidative process.

00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:34.694
The more yellow, the
closer it is to its best.

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:37.308
- As admitted amateur wine tasters,

00:01:37.308 --> 00:01:38.476
what should we be looking out for?

00:01:38.476 --> 00:01:41.136
- The best way for you
to develop your pallets,

00:01:41.136 --> 00:01:42.369
is to drink a lot of wine.

00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:43.202
(laughing)

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:45.355
So you've got a very happy
future in front of yourselves.

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:48.087
- We're starting off
with the 2016 Marsanne

00:01:48.087 --> 00:01:52.313
from the number one Marsanne
supplier in the world.

00:01:52.313 --> 00:01:53.489
- Pale white wines are one of the most

00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:55.730
beautiful colors in the natural world.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.635
To smell. You have to smell
wine before you drink it.

00:01:57.635 --> 00:01:59.222
This is a--
- I'm a seasoned wine taster.

00:01:59.222 --> 00:02:00.055
- You are...

00:02:00.055 --> 00:02:01.268
(glasses clinking)

00:02:01.268 --> 00:02:03.077
Look how fogged up your glass is.

00:02:03.077 --> 00:02:04.123
Seasoned my--

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:04.956
(beep)

00:02:04.956 --> 00:02:05.873
- Whatever.

00:02:06.805 --> 00:02:08.499
- Honestly, Halloween candy.

00:02:08.499 --> 00:02:12.068
It's like Starburst and
Snickers. Tell me what you smell.

00:02:12.068 --> 00:02:14.001
- I'm like, taking a
nap at the beach, right?

00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:16.530
Guy's coming by with the cart of fruit.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.059
The pineapple, and the

00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:21.114
the oranges and the citrus fruits.

00:02:21.114 --> 00:02:22.508
You wanna drink?

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:23.341
- Ooh.

00:02:23.341 --> 00:02:26.009
When I pick up a wine that's this color,

00:02:26.009 --> 00:02:29.328
I'm expecting a sharpness,
so this is extremely smooth.

00:02:29.328 --> 00:02:31.280
- Words that come to mind:

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.861
sweet, oddly very
complex. Complexly sweet.

00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:37.623
- There's something fun about
tasting wine where it's made.

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:39.641
I mean we're in this cool, old building...

00:02:39.641 --> 00:02:41.480
- It's almost like you're tasting the--

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.313
- History!

00:02:42.313 --> 00:02:43.151
- The environment.

00:02:43.151 --> 00:02:44.717
- Oh, sure. I'm gonna get you a new glass.

00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:46.110
- We were encouraged to actually try

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.662
the Marsanne what it was like in 2010.

00:02:48.662 --> 00:02:51.889
- In 2010, there were
grapes that were crushed

00:02:51.889 --> 00:02:52.993
that turned into this.

00:02:52.993 --> 00:02:54.899
- I was the baby.

00:02:54.899 --> 00:02:56.847
- How old are you?

00:02:56.847 --> 00:02:59.045
- Alrighty. Time travel, 2010, here we go.

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:01.460
(glasses clinking)

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.830
- Smell it! Jesus, have
you learned nothing?

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.034
Woah. That is a drastically
different smell.

00:03:08.034 --> 00:03:09.825
- This is mature.

00:03:09.825 --> 00:03:11.142
That's nice.

00:03:11.142 --> 00:03:12.223
It's muskier.

00:03:12.223 --> 00:03:13.331
- You know what this makes me think of?

00:03:13.331 --> 00:03:16.162
You're a kid, put a
rubber band in your mouth.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.305
If you reduced all of the akridness

00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:20.093
and just had that pure taste...

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:22.247
- You're spot on with that rubber band.

00:03:22.247 --> 00:03:24.458
- It's like a little
piney, like, pine cone-y.

00:03:24.458 --> 00:03:26.779
I bet a rubber tree smells and tastes

00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:29.100
exactly like this wine,
in a delicious way.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.269
- I know that sounds crazy.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:33.602
It sounds crazy to you, Adam? Come here.

00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:36.607
- [Steven] How was it?

00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:37.975
- [Andrew] Was I wrong?

00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:38.808
Thank you.

00:03:38.808 --> 00:03:41.993
Mellow, fruity, delight of a wine.

00:03:41.993 --> 00:03:43.672
- And dare I say, truffle-y.

00:03:43.672 --> 00:03:44.932
- It was not truffle-y.

00:03:44.932 --> 00:03:46.337
- Slightly truffle-y.

00:03:46.337 --> 00:03:47.170
Wine fact!

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.413
Grapes native to Australia
are not suitable for wine.

00:03:50.413 --> 00:03:51.287
- Really?

00:03:51.287 --> 00:03:52.828
- The wine industry in Australia

00:03:52.828 --> 00:03:55.477
actually comes from
immigrants from Europe.

00:03:55.477 --> 00:03:58.158
Those people then export
it back to Europe.

00:03:58.158 --> 00:04:00.151
Transcontinental trade at its finest.

00:04:00.151 --> 00:04:02.061
- Wow, that is an interesting wine fact.

00:04:02.061 --> 00:04:03.090
- Wait, do you wanna hear
more about that fact?

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:03.923
There's more.

00:04:03.923 --> 00:04:04.756
- Not really.

00:04:04.756 --> 00:04:06.050
- There's more!

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.992
(jazz music)

00:04:08.992 --> 00:04:10.774
- My name's Ian Firth,
I'm the chief storyteller

00:04:10.774 --> 00:04:11.827
here at Fowle's wine.

00:04:11.827 --> 00:04:13.955
- What does that mean exactly?

00:04:13.955 --> 00:04:15.766
- It means that I have absolutely no

00:04:15.766 --> 00:04:16.953
qualifications whatsoever.

00:04:16.953 --> 00:04:17.786
(laughing)

00:04:17.786 --> 00:04:19.638
I'm not a winemaker, I'm
not a good acculturalist,

00:04:19.638 --> 00:04:22.053
I just love wine and I
love talking about wine.

00:04:22.053 --> 00:04:23.381
Today we're gonna be trying the

00:04:23.381 --> 00:04:25.048
Ladies who Shoot their Lunch Shiraz.

00:04:25.048 --> 00:04:26.274
- [Steven] Ladies who Shoot their Lunch.

00:04:26.274 --> 00:04:27.401
Is there a story behind that?

00:04:27.401 --> 00:04:28.442
- [Ian] There is a story about that.

00:04:28.442 --> 00:04:29.985
Our chief winemaker is an avid hunter.

00:04:29.985 --> 00:04:31.948
He was eating these
meats and trying to match

00:04:31.948 --> 00:04:33.394
the right wine to it and he thought,

00:04:33.394 --> 00:04:35.548
"There's gotta be a wine
that's perfect for game food."

00:04:35.548 --> 00:04:38.172
- What specific foods did
you have in mind with these?

00:04:38.172 --> 00:04:39.949
- [Ian] So at the moment
we're matching a venison

00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:41.695
with the Shiraz, with a beetroot relish.

00:04:41.695 --> 00:04:43.472
You get this wonderful burst of flavor

00:04:43.472 --> 00:04:45.975
between the two that just
compliment each other really well.

00:04:45.975 --> 00:04:48.006
Fowles wine is located in
the Strathbogie Ranges.

00:04:48.006 --> 00:04:49.625
This is a cool climate region now,

00:04:49.625 --> 00:04:51.748
vineyards are at 500
meters above sea level.

00:04:51.748 --> 00:04:53.093
It's a very even ripening process,

00:04:53.093 --> 00:04:55.036
so you get really great ripe fruit,

00:04:55.036 --> 00:04:57.267
but you maintain some really
lovely acid with the wine.

00:04:57.267 --> 00:04:58.635
- And that's because of the weather

00:04:58.635 --> 00:05:00.082
you have in this particular area?

00:05:00.082 --> 00:05:02.083
- Absolutely, because
of that cooler climate

00:05:02.083 --> 00:05:05.080
the fruit has time to develop
these really intense flavors.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.437
We ferment it in stainless
steel, to start off with,

00:05:07.437 --> 00:05:10.322
and we retain half of that
batch in stainless steel

00:05:10.322 --> 00:05:12.536
to keep that primary fruit character.

00:05:12.536 --> 00:05:16.036
25% of the batch goes into
very traditional oak barrels,

00:05:16.036 --> 00:05:18.950
so you do get a lot of
new oak flavor coming in.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:23.317
The last 25% we put into
140 year old English casks.

00:05:23.317 --> 00:05:25.965
Those three components, and
then blended back together

00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:27.279
to create the final wine.

00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:29.017
- That's some impressive
chemistry happening there.

00:05:29.017 --> 00:05:30.022
- Shall we try some?

00:05:30.022 --> 00:05:33.105
(lively regal music)

00:05:35.679 --> 00:05:39.142
- I can only start this
taste test by just laughing.

00:05:39.142 --> 00:05:41.782
- Why? Cos it's the most
romantic setting possible?

00:05:41.782 --> 00:05:44.066
Two glasses of wine, a baguette.

00:05:44.066 --> 00:05:46.624
Let's just take a moment
and appreciate this.

00:05:46.624 --> 00:05:49.707
(lively regal music)

00:05:51.321 --> 00:05:52.154
- Alright.

00:05:52.154 --> 00:05:53.617
- To the boys who drink their lunch.

00:05:53.617 --> 00:05:56.375
(glasses clinking)

00:05:56.375 --> 00:05:57.370
- That's delightful.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:58.203
- That's gorgeous.

00:05:58.203 --> 00:06:00.455
(lively regal music)

00:06:00.455 --> 00:06:01.702
A little speechless right now

00:06:01.702 --> 00:06:04.368
by just the wine, the countryside,

00:06:04.368 --> 00:06:06.346
there's a baby over there
hanging out with us.

00:06:06.346 --> 00:06:07.862
- What sort of mouth movement should I do

00:06:07.862 --> 00:06:11.716
when tasting a wine for the first time?

00:06:11.716 --> 00:06:13.260
- [Ian] Suck the air in
over the top of your tongue,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.520
which aerates the wine.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.624
- Let's taste our wine for real this time.

00:06:16.624 --> 00:06:18.291
(glasses clinking)

00:06:18.291 --> 00:06:20.541
(slurping)

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:22.803
That's like a whole other wine.

00:06:22.803 --> 00:06:24.941
- Okay, I got exactly
what that tastes like now.

00:06:24.941 --> 00:06:26.950
It's fruity, it dances on your tongue,

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.000
it actually makes you
thirstier the more you drink.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:29.875
(glasses clink)

00:06:29.875 --> 00:06:30.833
Up yours.

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:33.728
(laughing)

00:06:33.728 --> 00:06:36.511
This is a dangerous wine.

00:06:36.511 --> 00:06:40.477
- Wine is often regarded as a
pretentious beverage, some--

00:06:40.477 --> 00:06:42.782
He knows what I'm talking about.

00:06:42.782 --> 00:06:45.922
Something that's enjoyed
in lounges, but this

00:06:45.922 --> 00:06:48.853
is in fact the proper setting
for wine to be enjoyed.

00:06:48.853 --> 00:06:50.479
It's not for dark dining rooms,

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.719
it's for bright, crisp vistas.

00:06:52.719 --> 00:06:54.653
- And the alcohol is definitely there,

00:06:54.653 --> 00:06:57.288
I can tell you that right now
because I'm feeling great.

00:06:57.288 --> 00:06:58.870
So here we have venison.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.638
- Deer, for the uninitiated,

00:07:00.638 --> 00:07:02.808
with a little beetroot chutney on top.

00:07:02.808 --> 00:07:03.641
- Cheers.

00:07:03.641 --> 00:07:07.412
(lively regal music)

00:07:07.412 --> 00:07:09.147
Damn, that is good.

00:07:09.147 --> 00:07:10.737
Here we go.

00:07:10.737 --> 00:07:12.225
- Oh man, that's beautiful.

00:07:12.225 --> 00:07:13.249
- Ooh!

00:07:13.249 --> 00:07:17.705
- I love this. It makes wine
seem a little bit more rugged

00:07:17.705 --> 00:07:19.126
and connected to the earth in a way

00:07:19.126 --> 00:07:20.895
that I've never appreciated before.

00:07:20.895 --> 00:07:23.063
- This countryside is a wonderland.

00:07:23.063 --> 00:07:26.063
- Kind of have a crush on this lady.

00:07:30.568 --> 00:07:32.411
The boys who drink their lunch.

00:07:32.411 --> 00:07:33.361
- Wine fact!

00:07:33.361 --> 00:07:35.918
Australia actually exports more wine

00:07:35.918 --> 00:07:37.427
to the UK than France does.

00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:38.635
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:07:38.635 --> 00:07:39.926
- Australians seem like they would

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:41.162
share anything with anybody.

00:07:41.162 --> 00:07:42.828
Made a bunch of wine--
- That's true.

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:43.667
- come have it.

00:07:43.667 --> 00:07:45.219
The French, on the other hand, seem like,

00:07:45.219 --> 00:07:46.779
"Oh I just keep it in my cellar.

00:07:46.779 --> 00:07:48.629
No one drinks it forever."

00:07:48.629 --> 00:07:49.462
(laughing)

00:07:49.462 --> 00:07:51.123
- Hey look, some cows.

00:07:51.123 --> 00:07:52.511
Sheep! Hold up.

00:07:52.511 --> 00:07:53.731
- Steven loves sheep.

00:07:53.731 --> 00:07:55.498
- Oh, not enough space on my phone.

00:07:55.498 --> 00:07:56.556
- Womp, womp.

00:07:56.556 --> 00:07:59.973
(bright classical music)

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.626
- My name is Nick, I'm a
winemaker at All Saints Estate,

00:08:05.626 --> 00:08:07.506
and one of the owners
along with my two sisters.

00:08:07.506 --> 00:08:10.339
Today we're tasting the Museum
Muscadelle and Museum Muscat,

00:08:10.339 --> 00:08:13.346
which are both fortified wines,
upon average 100 years old.

00:08:13.346 --> 00:08:16.081
Fortified wine is a wine
that is fortified by spirit,

00:08:16.081 --> 00:08:17.848
in our case, neutral grape spirit.

00:08:17.848 --> 00:08:19.633
- So you're adding a stronger alcohol

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:22.042
to a wine that's not done becoming a wine.

00:08:22.042 --> 00:08:22.875
- Correct.

00:08:22.875 --> 00:08:24.906
The grapes, they're on the
vine ripening 'til Autumn

00:08:24.906 --> 00:08:27.191
where the sugar
accumulation is super high.

00:08:27.191 --> 00:08:28.863
Before the yeast gobble up all the sugar

00:08:28.863 --> 00:08:32.035
and turn it into alcohol,
we pump in 96% grape spirit,

00:08:32.035 --> 00:08:33.387
which stops the yeast in their tracks,

00:08:33.387 --> 00:08:36.728
increases the alcohol to about
18%, and fortifies the wine.

00:08:36.728 --> 00:08:38.117
So the wines we're tasting today

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:40.002
are a blend of different years,

00:08:40.002 --> 00:08:41.781
but on average it's 100 years old.

00:08:41.781 --> 00:08:43.598
- [Steven] You're actually
inheriting somebody

00:08:43.598 --> 00:08:46.308
who had the foresight
to barrel up the wine

00:08:46.308 --> 00:08:47.845
and let it sit for 100 years.

00:08:47.845 --> 00:08:48.678
- [Nick] Yeah, that's right.

00:08:48.678 --> 00:08:50.758
- [Andrew] Are you presently creating wine

00:08:50.758 --> 00:08:54.323
that 100 years in the future
then also turn into this wine?

00:08:54.323 --> 00:08:55.156
- [Nick] That's right.

00:08:55.156 --> 00:08:56.586
- [Andrew] Kind of a weird
thing to think about, isn't it?

00:08:56.586 --> 00:08:57.753
- [Nick] It is, yeah, it's a wine that

00:08:57.753 --> 00:08:59.686
no one will drink whilst I'm alive.

00:08:59.686 --> 00:09:01.624
- How do you arrive at
the combination of wines

00:09:01.624 --> 00:09:03.675
that go into this to form the blend?

00:09:03.675 --> 00:09:06.160
- There might be 30 or 40 wines lined up.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.904
We smell and taste through the wines

00:09:07.904 --> 00:09:09.844
and choose the final blend.

00:09:09.844 --> 00:09:12.316
It's such an amazing,
complex style of wine.

00:09:12.316 --> 00:09:13.328
One sip goes a long way.

00:09:13.328 --> 00:09:14.241
- So how much is this wine?

00:09:14.241 --> 00:09:16.736
- About $1,000. It is
a lot of money, I know.

00:09:16.736 --> 00:09:18.623
My sisters and I actually
asked our accountant

00:09:18.623 --> 00:09:21.681
if he could work out what
the wine costs us to make.

00:09:21.681 --> 00:09:24.313
He came back with a figure
of $20,000 a bottle.

00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:25.146
- [Andrew] Really?
- [Steven] What?

00:09:25.146 --> 00:09:26.707
- By adding up all the time it's taken

00:09:26.707 --> 00:09:29.181
to look after the wine,
and my sisters and I said

00:09:29.181 --> 00:09:30.727
we can't charge that but,

00:09:30.727 --> 00:09:34.133
$1,000 should cover
our costs and our time.

00:09:34.133 --> 00:09:36.388
- Imagine a $20,000 new car

00:09:36.388 --> 00:09:38.160
that you could have for $1,000.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:38.993
- Yeah.

00:09:38.993 --> 00:09:41.602
We are going to now try two $1,000 wines.

00:09:41.602 --> 00:09:44.059
- [Nick] This one is the All
Saints Museum Muscadelle.

00:09:44.059 --> 00:09:47.310
Muscadelle's more aromatic
and tastes a bit lighter.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.705
(bright classical music)

00:09:49.705 --> 00:09:50.663
- [Steven] I never experienced

00:09:50.663 --> 00:09:51.804
that much wine by just sniffing.

00:09:51.804 --> 00:09:53.377
- At first it reminded me of when

00:09:53.377 --> 00:09:55.215
I discovered my mother's vanilla extract

00:09:55.215 --> 00:09:57.019
and I was like, "This will be delicious."

00:09:57.019 --> 00:09:57.853
- Let me set the scene.

00:09:57.853 --> 00:09:59.877
You're in Harry Potter's world.

00:09:59.877 --> 00:10:00.710
- Okay.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.064
- And you walk into the
chocolate candy shop.

00:10:03.064 --> 00:10:03.897
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:10:03.897 --> 00:10:05.843
- And there's just all these ridiculously

00:10:05.843 --> 00:10:08.061
amazing candies on the wall.

00:10:08.061 --> 00:10:10.661
- This would for sure be
Dumbledore's preferred drink.

00:10:10.661 --> 00:10:11.494
- Cheers.

00:10:11.494 --> 00:10:12.569
(glasses clinking)

00:10:12.569 --> 00:10:15.171
(bright classical music)

00:10:15.171 --> 00:10:17.888
- Oh my god. Oh my god.

00:10:17.888 --> 00:10:19.060
- [Andrew] That's extremely tasty.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.907
- I don't wanna sound pretentious.

00:10:20.907 --> 00:10:23.461
Like, it was the deepest, richest,

00:10:23.461 --> 00:10:26.304
grape-y, raisin flavor, just--

00:10:26.304 --> 00:10:27.137
- So raisin-y.

00:10:27.137 --> 00:10:27.970
- Filling.

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.037
- It's like you had the most
delicious banquet dinner.

00:10:31.037 --> 00:10:32.671
The moment after your last bite

00:10:32.671 --> 00:10:35.115
and you're reminded of all
the flavors you just had.

00:10:35.115 --> 00:10:37.037
It's all of those in an instant.

00:10:37.037 --> 00:10:39.678
- And it has the touch of a lover.

00:10:39.678 --> 00:10:41.013
- I kind of get what you mean.

00:10:41.013 --> 00:10:42.204
- Yes!
- Yes!

00:10:42.204 --> 00:10:44.266
- [Nick] This one is the
All Saints Museum Muscat.

00:10:44.266 --> 00:10:46.946
Richer and more concentrated,
and they say it to be sweeter.

00:10:46.946 --> 00:10:47.779
- Woah.

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:50.148
- [Andrew] This is a step
up from the previous one.

00:10:50.148 --> 00:10:51.223
- [Steven] This is deep.

00:10:51.223 --> 00:10:53.869
- Look how steamy you made
your glass with the smelling.

00:10:53.869 --> 00:10:55.149
You're a monster.

00:10:55.149 --> 00:10:59.214
(bright classical music)

00:10:59.214 --> 00:11:00.672
- Oh yeah.
- Oh yeah.

00:11:00.672 --> 00:11:03.554
That one was more fragrant,
this one's more intense.

00:11:03.554 --> 00:11:05.597
- It's like this labyrinth of flavor.

00:11:05.597 --> 00:11:07.727
That is why it would be
Dumbledore's favorite drink.

00:11:07.727 --> 00:11:10.414
You know when he pulls the
memories out of his mind?

00:11:10.414 --> 00:11:14.217
This is like that time warp
where you can just sample

00:11:14.217 --> 00:11:16.325
an infinite number of memories.

00:11:16.325 --> 00:11:18.401
- It's ticking the top
of the roof of my mouth,

00:11:18.401 --> 00:11:20.361
but I don't have taste buds up
there, how is that possible?

00:11:20.361 --> 00:11:22.145
I'm having a hard time
coming up with the words

00:11:22.145 --> 00:11:25.094
to describe how powerful
and beautiful this is.

00:11:25.094 --> 00:11:26.299
- I think that's important, though.

00:11:26.299 --> 00:11:29.160
If it was easier to explain,
it wouldn't be as worth it.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.480
- Somebody was preparing this wine

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.798
before I was alive,
before my mom was alive...

00:11:32.798 --> 00:11:35.905
- But they didn't know
that it would make this.

00:11:35.905 --> 00:11:37.315
And that is the crazy part.

00:11:37.315 --> 00:11:39.322
I'm still not sure I know
totally what it tastes like,

00:11:39.322 --> 00:11:42.905
but it tastes like a
lot and I'm liking it.

00:11:48.123 --> 00:11:49.143
- That's crazy.

00:11:49.143 --> 00:11:51.628
- Which wine was the most
worth it at its price range?

00:11:51.628 --> 00:11:53.932
- The Marsanne Tahbilk was unlike

00:11:53.932 --> 00:11:55.277
any other white wine I've ever had.

00:11:55.277 --> 00:11:58.287
Fowles, the Ladies who Shoot their Lunch.

00:11:58.287 --> 00:11:59.534
That was my Worth It winner.

00:11:59.534 --> 00:12:01.391
- Of course, that lady
on the bottle got you.

00:12:01.391 --> 00:12:03.535
- Did she snipe my heart?

00:12:03.535 --> 00:12:04.368
Perhaps.

00:12:04.368 --> 00:12:07.011
It was just the most delicious,
and I really like the label.

00:12:07.011 --> 00:12:09.129
And to be honest that's
how I pick most of my wine.

00:12:09.129 --> 00:12:10.879
Who's your winner?

00:12:10.879 --> 00:12:12.734
- They were just all so good.

00:12:12.734 --> 00:12:15.098
I think I'm gonna go with All Saints wine.

00:12:15.098 --> 00:12:15.931
- [Andrew] Really?

00:12:15.931 --> 00:12:19.578
- Yes! I can't believe it!
I'm not a fancy boy I promise.

00:12:19.578 --> 00:12:22.254
That wine was born more
than 100 years ago.

00:12:22.254 --> 00:12:23.815
Even taste the history.

00:12:23.815 --> 00:12:26.734
That does it for Worth
It Wine, Oz edition.

00:12:26.734 --> 00:12:29.352
- You guys aren't gonna
ask about my favorite wine?

00:12:29.352 --> 00:12:31.968
- What's your favorite wine?

00:12:31.968 --> 00:12:33.034
- Ladies who Shoot their Lunch.

00:12:33.034 --> 00:12:33.867
- I win.

00:12:33.867 --> 00:12:36.398
- So this was only part two
of a three part Oz series.

00:12:36.398 --> 00:12:39.004
So, we've got something special coming up.

00:12:39.004 --> 00:12:41.621
Maybe with some of the
background of this shot actually.

00:12:41.621 --> 00:12:43.480
Rhymes with rake.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:44.392
(laughing)

00:12:44.392 --> 00:12:46.360
Cos we're rakin' in the cows.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:47.693
- Rhymes with...

00:12:48.944 --> 00:12:49.926
Cake.

00:12:49.926 --> 00:12:51.268
It's not cake, though.

00:12:51.268 --> 00:12:52.601
- [Male] Oh yes!

